WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.720
Hey, welcome to the team!

00:01.720 --> 00:06.480
Today, we're gonna break down what is, without a doubt, the single most important conversation

00:06.480 --> 00:07.600
you'll have with a homeowner.

00:07.600 --> 00:09.620
It's the post-inspection pitch.

00:09.620 --> 00:14.380
This is that magic moment where all your hard work up on that roof turns into a signed claim.

00:14.380 --> 00:16.000
So let's get right into it.

00:16.000 --> 00:17.160
So, picture this.

00:17.160 --> 00:21.200
You're climbing down the ladder, your tablet is loaded with photos, and you know for a

00:21.200 --> 00:24.100
fact you've found legitimate storm damage.

00:24.100 --> 00:25.960
This is the moment of truth.

00:25.960 --> 00:27.080
What happens next?

00:27.080 --> 00:29.560
Are you just another salesperson trying to make a sale?

00:29.560 --> 00:33.280
Or are you the expert who's about to solve a major problem for this homeowner?

00:33.280 --> 00:37.920
Seriously, what you say in the next 10 minutes, it makes all the difference in the world.

00:37.920 --> 00:41.400
So we're gonna walk through the five essential parts of this conversation.

00:41.400 --> 00:42.680
Think of it as your playbook.

00:42.680 --> 00:45.380
First, we'll set the stage for success.

00:45.380 --> 00:49.840
Then we'll build our case, present the core evidence, pivot to become their advocate,

00:49.840 --> 00:52.320
and finally, lock in that next step.

00:52.320 --> 00:56.680
Alright, let's kick things off with part one, the winning pitch.

00:56.680 --> 01:01.400
You know, success here doesn't actually start when you knock on the door to show your findings.

01:01.400 --> 01:02.400
Nope.

01:02.400 --> 01:04.920
It starts while you're still up on the roof.

01:04.920 --> 01:08.000
Those photos you're taking, they aren't just for your records.

01:08.000 --> 01:11.000
They are the entire foundation of your entire pitch.

01:11.000 --> 01:13.600
Look, this is not just some boring checklist.

01:13.600 --> 01:16.160
This is your evidence-gathering battle plan.

01:16.160 --> 01:19.720
We are not just taking random pictures of dings and dents, we are building a visual

01:19.720 --> 01:20.720
story.

01:20.720 --> 01:24.280
We need clear photos of the collateral damage on the ground level, damage to the roof metals

01:24.280 --> 01:28.640
and vents, hail and wind hits that you've clearly marked up, and, this one's a biggie,

01:28.640 --> 01:30.740
the granules you find in the gutters.

01:30.740 --> 01:34.040
Every single one of these photos is a crucial piece of the puzzle you're about to put

01:34.040 --> 01:35.600
together for the homeowner.

01:35.600 --> 01:39.680
Okay, so you've got your evidence, you're at the door, you're ready to present your

01:39.680 --> 01:40.680
case.

01:40.680 --> 01:41.680
So how do you start?

01:41.680 --> 01:42.920
Well, here's the key.

01:42.920 --> 01:45.040
You don't actually start with the roof.

01:45.040 --> 01:49.120
It's all about how you frame the conversation from your very first sense.

01:49.120 --> 01:53.320
Your opening move is to show them pictures of damage to things other than their roof.

01:53.360 --> 01:54.760
I'm serious.

01:54.760 --> 01:58.200
You start with the dings on the gutters, the little tears in their window screens, the

01:58.200 --> 01:59.200
dents on the downspots.

01:59.200 --> 02:03.720
Now, I know it might seem backward, but trust me, it is a powerful way to establish the

02:03.720 --> 02:07.320
facts of the storm before you even mention the main event.

02:07.320 --> 02:11.040
Now, here is the exact phrasing that just works wonders.

02:11.040 --> 02:13.000
You're going to say something just like this.

02:13.000 --> 02:17.360
Look, while this damage to your gutters and screens functionally isn't a huge deal, it

02:17.360 --> 02:19.360
really helps us build a story.

02:19.400 --> 02:23.320
I want you to think of us like lawyers and this collateral damage right here.

02:23.320 --> 02:27.880
This is the undisputed evidence that builds our case to get your roof approved.

02:27.880 --> 02:28.880
See that?

02:28.880 --> 02:33.560
The tone is confident, it's educational, and it immediately positions you as the expert.

02:33.560 --> 02:37.600
So why is that lawyer analogy so ridiculously effective?

02:37.600 --> 02:41.800
Well, because it flips the entire dynamic on its head.

02:41.800 --> 02:44.920
In one sentence, you are no longer a salesperson.

02:44.920 --> 02:49.320
You are an expert advocate building a case on their behalf.

02:49.320 --> 02:53.520
You've proven the storm was real with evidence nobody can argue with, which makes the damage

02:53.520 --> 02:57.760
on the roof feel like the logical, inevitable conclusion to the story.

02:57.760 --> 03:03.240
Okay, you have totally set the stage and established yourself as their expert.

03:03.240 --> 03:08.120
Now it's time to pivot to the main event, presenting the core evidence you found on

03:08.120 --> 03:10.120
the roof itself.

03:10.120 --> 03:13.800
Your explanation here has to be simple and crystal clear.

03:13.800 --> 03:18.000
You'll show them the photos and say, okay, here's the damage to your shingles.

03:18.000 --> 03:20.240
And I've made it really easy to see.

03:20.240 --> 03:24.160
Anything I've circled is hail damage, and anything I've put a slash through is wind

03:24.160 --> 03:25.260
damage.

03:25.260 --> 03:29.060
That simple system makes it instantly understandable for any homeowner.

03:29.060 --> 03:30.060
No confusion.

03:30.060 --> 03:34.240
Now, listen, it's not enough to just point at a circle on a picture.

03:34.240 --> 03:36.920
You have to explain the implication.

03:36.920 --> 03:39.060
Use this exact explanation.

03:39.060 --> 03:43.740
You know, this little divot might not look like much right now, but here's what happens.

03:43.740 --> 03:48.380
Whatever gets in there, then it freezes and expands over and over, and that process just

03:48.380 --> 03:51.140
breaks the shingle apart from the inside out.

03:51.140 --> 03:54.680
That's what eventually leads to leaks, and that is exactly why your insurance policy

03:54.680 --> 03:56.420
covers this.

03:56.420 --> 03:59.580
Next up, you're going to show them those photos of the granules in the gutters.

03:59.580 --> 04:02.700
I'm telling you, this is powerful, tangible proof.

04:02.700 --> 04:05.260
You'll say something like, see all this stuff in your gutter?

04:05.260 --> 04:08.740
These are the protective granules that are supposed to be on top of your shingles.

04:08.740 --> 04:12.100
When a storm knocks them off like this, it's basically like taking the sunscreen off your

04:12.100 --> 04:13.100
roof.

04:13.100 --> 04:14.980
It automatically shortens its lifespan.

04:14.980 --> 04:17.580
Now for a step that builds a ton of confidence.

04:17.580 --> 04:19.780
You connect their damage to their neighbors.

04:19.780 --> 04:23.140
You can say something like, you know, this is very similar to the damage we found on

04:23.140 --> 04:27.060
the Smith's home just down the street, and we were actually able to get their claim fully

04:27.060 --> 04:28.380
approved.

04:28.380 --> 04:30.220
This is powerful social proof.

04:30.220 --> 04:33.580
It lets them know they're not alone, and that there's a clear, proven path to getting

04:33.580 --> 04:34.580
this fixed.

04:34.580 --> 04:38.540
Okay, you've laid out all the evidence like a total pro.

04:38.540 --> 04:41.500
Now comes the crucial pivot in the conversation.

04:41.500 --> 04:46.260
This is where you shift from being just the inspector to becoming their absolutely indispensable

04:46.260 --> 04:49.380
advocate for the whole insurance process.

04:49.380 --> 04:53.660
This next part of the script is all about aligning yourself with the homeowner against

04:53.660 --> 04:54.660
the problem.

04:54.660 --> 04:59.700
You'll say, now, let's be honest, insurance companies are in the business of minimizing

04:59.700 --> 05:00.700
payouts.

05:00.700 --> 05:02.060
It's just how their business model works.

05:02.060 --> 05:05.980
So the most important part of this entire process is that when their adjuster comes

05:05.980 --> 05:10.900
out, we are here as the storm damage experts to make sure you, the homeowner, get a completely

05:10.900 --> 05:12.460
fair shake.

05:12.460 --> 05:15.180
Let's break down why that is so effective.

05:15.180 --> 05:18.900
In one simple move, you have completely reframed the relationship.

05:18.900 --> 05:22.020
It's no longer you and the homeowner sitting across the table.

05:22.020 --> 05:27.460
It is now you and the homeowner on the exact same team, working together.

05:27.460 --> 05:31.380
This instantly creates a partnership and makes your presence at the adjuster meeting feel

05:31.380 --> 05:34.460
not just helpful, but absolutely essential.

05:34.460 --> 05:38.060
So you've built your case, you've formed an alliance.

05:38.060 --> 05:40.820
Right now, you have all the momentum.

05:40.820 --> 05:45.500
This final step is all about turning that momentum into action by confidently guiding

05:45.500 --> 05:48.140
the homeowner to the next logical step.

05:48.140 --> 05:51.820
This is not the time to be timid and ask, so what do you think?

05:51.820 --> 05:54.100
You've already established yourself as the expert.

05:54.100 --> 05:56.340
Now they're looking to you for direction.

05:56.340 --> 06:00.060
You have to lead with a confident, action-oriented statement.

06:00.060 --> 06:02.620
This is the line that moves everything forward.

06:02.620 --> 06:05.580
Notice it's not a question, it's a statement of action.

06:05.580 --> 06:09.980
What I'm going to do now is run to my car, grab my iPad, and we can get this started.

06:09.980 --> 06:12.220
Then you just turn and start walking to your car.

06:12.220 --> 06:15.700
As you're walking back, that's when you ask, hey, is there a good place we could sit down

06:15.700 --> 06:17.500
for just about five minutes?

06:17.500 --> 06:21.460
You are guiding the entire process with simple, confident steps.

06:21.460 --> 06:25.720
Once you're inside, the process is simple, and more importantly, it's repeatable.

06:25.720 --> 06:29.740
You sit down, you build a little more rapport, you gather their basic information right there

06:29.740 --> 06:33.780
on the iPad, and then you guide them through making the initial call to their insurance

06:33.780 --> 06:34.780
company.

06:34.780 --> 06:38.500
Just follow these steps every single time, and you'll be golden.

06:38.540 --> 06:43.220
I want you to know this entire strategy from the lawyer analogy all the way to these final

06:43.220 --> 06:47.460
steps, it's a direct reflection of our core company values, integrity in how you build

06:47.460 --> 06:52.000
your case, quality in the evidence you gather, and simplicity in how you guide the homeowner.

06:52.000 --> 06:55.500
This isn't just a sales pitch, it's genuinely how we operate.

06:55.500 --> 06:57.060
You now have the playbook.

06:57.060 --> 07:01.140
You have the words, you have the strategy, and you have the framework to turn every valid

07:01.140 --> 07:04.320
inspection into a real solution for the homeowner.

07:04.320 --> 07:07.200
This is how you build trust, and this is how you win.

07:07.200 --> 07:11.320
The only question left is, are you ready to go out there and be the expert they need?

